slides.body:
JavaScript that reads + writes the DOM in a tight loop can trigger layout multiple times per frame:

```js
for (const el of items) {
  el.style.width = el.offsetWidth + 10 + 'px';
}
```

The trap: `offsetWidth` is a *read* that forces layout (it needs current geometry). `style.width = …` is a *write* that invalidates layout. Inside the loop: write → next-iteration read forces layout immediately → write again → repeat. $N$ items → $N$ layouts, not 1.

The fix: batch reads + writes:
```js
const widths = items.map((el) => el.offsetWidth + 10);
items.forEach((el, i) => { el.style.width = widths[i] + 'px'; });
```

One layout instead of $N$. The pattern is so common that React, Vue, and Solid all batch DOM mutations through a single 'commit phase' — preventing this class of bug by construction.
